{"kind":"request","text":"Roles as viewpoints: amend the roles note, and the tasks that follow.\n\nDirection, 30 October 2026. Participants in a team or a matter are present because they hold different viewpoints on the domain, more than different skills: the architect, the security engineer and the operator of one release. Roles are central. For a person, a role carries informal text or formal requirements (\"certified engineer\") that guide discovery and responsibilities. For an agent, a role carries a mechanical description: skills, prompts and other context. The landed boundary-objects note (notes/2026-10-28-boundary-objects-in-use.md, main 25930f62) does not change for this.\n\n1. What the record holds today\n\n- Authority is a roster role: a list of actions, in a fixed code table of five roles (admin, maintainer, member, agent, checker) in packages/platform/src/membership.ts (ROLE_TABLE_1 and version 2). Every entry observes the signer's role and actions (packages/contract/src/observation.ts). The 26 October decision carried by request a455fdba makes the roster table a membership version held as data.\n- Description exists only for agents, as the role preset of notes/2026-10-21-roles-flow-and-instances.md section 2: authority (kind, controller, actions), behaviour (a role revision of model, system prompt and defaults, plus skills) and flow (candidate reads as item queries, eligibility checks, admit, performs, hands off). \"Where a preset lives\" places it in host configuration, outside the record. notes/2026-10-15-hosted-agent-teams-design.md keeps model and system prompt as host role configuration and makes a role revision immutable once made.\n- Packages declare default roles and their actions in the manifest (notes/2026-10-15-self-contained-applications.md lines 93, 109 and 246). They carry no description.\n- People have no description. A person's role is its action list.\n\n2. What the literature says\n\nStar and Griesemer (1989) is about this: amateur collectors, biologists, trappers, administrators and a philanthropist cooperated on the same specimens without agreeing what a specimen was, and no viewpoint was the obligatory passage point. Bowker and Star (1999) make a viewpoint a membership in a community of practice, that is a set of categories the holder has stopped seeing as categories. Appleton (2026) names prompts, skills and agent instruction files as the present boundary objects between people and agents. Webstrates' asymmetric collaboration is the mechanism form: one document, two editors, each the viewpoint of its user. Gawande's account (2018) is a viewpoint failure: the billing and compliance viewpoint's structure imposed on the clinical one, repaired by a department's own screen over the same record.\n\nThree treatments we have not read speak to the direction directly, and the planner reads them before amending: Carlile (2002), \"A pragmatic view of knowledge and boundaries\", which grades boundaries as syntactic, semantic and pragmatic and says that where interests differ a boundary object must support negotiation and transformation, not only representation; Boland and Tenkasi (1995), \"Perspective making and perspective taking in communities of knowing\", on teams that need strong perspectives inside each community and a way to see another's perspective without adopting it; and Finkelstein, Kramer, Nuseibeh, Finkelstein and Goedicke (1992), \"Viewpoints: a framework for integrating multiple perspectives in system development\", with Nuseibeh's \"To be and not to be: on managing inconsistency in software development\" (1996), where each participant keeps a partial specification in their own notation, consistency is checked only where a relationship is declared, and inconsistency is kept as information. ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 and Kruchten's 4+1 are the tame descendants.\n\n3. The design\n\nA role has two halves, and the record already keeps one of them. Authority stays in membership, because the fold reads it: the roster role, its actions, and the enrolment rules. The description is the viewpoint's own account of itself, and it belongs in the room as a note under the one commons act: a note whose media type says that it is a role description and whose about names the role's membership entry.\n\n- For a person the text is prose, informal or formal: \"reviews every change that touches the ledger\", or \"certified engineer; licence on file\", where the licence is a retained document that the member's enrolment cites.\n- For an agent the text is the behaviour and flow parts of today's preset, signed by the controller: model, system prompt, defaults, skills, candidate reads, eligibility checks, what the role performs and what it hands off. The authority part of the preset stays as it is. A preset never granted an action, and a description never will.\n- A revision is a new note by the same signer about the old, as the landed note's revision reading says for data.\n- A guard never reads a description. The actions and the enrolment rule are what a guard reads. A description naturalizes its own categories, and the other roles read it in theirs, which is right for a description and wrong for a rule.\n\nThree things follow without new mechanism.\n\n- Discovery is the reads. An agent's description lists candidate reads as item queries. For a person who holds the same role, the host draws those reads as the role's default view. This answers the one gap the boundary-objects discussion left: viewpoints inside one room have no home, because every member reads every scope and views are kept by individual people. A view's audience can be a role, and the role's reads are its first view.\n- Perspective taking is free. Every member reads every scope, so each role's description is readable by the other roles, which Boland and Tenkasi say is what teams of distinct perspectives most need.\n- Formal requirements are the same slope the landed note describes. An informal requirement is text in the description. A formal one is a rule on enrolment that reads a retained credential cited by the enrolment, under the evidence-rules design. The room moves from text to rule when it wants the record, and not the people, to refuse an unqualified holder.\n\nThe host records which description revision an instance ran under, in its assignment record (roles note section 3), so the record shows what an agent was told when it acted. The host keeps what is the host's: the list of allowed models, spending, supervision and recovery (agent-teams note section 6). A package may ship a default description beside each default role, and install proposes them as notes in the room.\n\n4. Tasks, in order\n\nT1. Read Carlile (2002), Boland and Tenkasi (1995) and the ViewPoints paper (1992) with Nuseibeh (1996). Carry what they change into T2 as a short source paragraph. No filing.\n\nT2. Amend notes/2026-10-21-roles-flow-and-instances.md. Section 1 gains the term role description. Section 2 \"Where a preset lives\" changes: the behaviour and flow parts live in the record as a role description note about the role's membership entry; a preset is the authority part and a reference to one description revision; the host checks a description against the installed app as it checks a preset today, and refuses an instance whose description names an act, item type or model the app or the host does not allow. Section 3 says that the assignment record names the description revision. Section 9 gains a witness: an instance acts under description revision 1, the controller writes revision 2, the next attempt runs under 2, and the record shows each attempt's revision. Section 10: RI2 and RI4 carry the description note; RI3 shows the role's description and its default view under the member badge and in the team view. A person's description is one sentence of scope in this note and a worked example (a reviewer role with an informal requirement).\n\nT3. Amend notes/2026-10-15-hosted-agent-teams-design.md: model and system prompt move from host role configuration to the record's role description; the \"Role configuration\" and \"Role revision\" rows of its tables say so; the host keeps the allowed-model list, spending, supervision and recovery. Section 6 does not change in substance.\n\nT4. Amend BO2 (request c4ccdba6) with one line: a view's audience may be a role, named in the view note's about, and the host draws a role description's reads as that role's default view. W2 gains one check: a member who holds the role sees the default view without writing anything.\n\nT5. Amend notes/2026-10-15-self-contained-applications.md: the manifest may carry a default role description beside each default role, and the install that proposes the default roles (line 246) proposes the descriptions as notes.\n\nT6. File one backlog item under the evidence-rules design: an enrolment requirement, a rule on membership.invite that reads a retained credential cited by the enrolment, with the refusal named. Not scheduled; the item records the formal end of the slope.\n\nT7. One roadmap row under agent teams: role descriptions in the record for people and agents, with the viewpoint framing in one sentence.\n\nT8. Order: T2 to T5 land as one planner head after the roles-as-data membership version lands and after RI1, and before RI2 is filed, so that RI2 carries the description from the start.\n\nWhat does not change: the landed boundary-objects note; the membership authority model; the fold reads no description; the commons stays one act.\n\nConditions: T2, T3 and T5 land on main as one planner head after checker review, with a source paragraph from T1; T4 is filed as an amendment to c4ccdba6; T6 and T7 are filed; the head's status lines match main; this request files no implementation request of its own.","body":{"conditions":"Roles note, agent-teams note and self-contained applications note amended for role descriptions in the record (people: text or requirements; agents: behaviour and flow of the preset) and landed as one planner head after checker review, with a source paragraph from Carlile 2002, Boland and Tenkasi 1995 and the ViewPoints papers; BO2 c4ccdba6 amended for role-scoped default views; an enrolment-requirement backlog item under evidence rules and one roadmap row filed; status lines match main; no implementation request filed by this request.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}